 Ordinary Differential Operators: Vectors and Functions 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Finally, we can multiply functions by scalars, just like we do for vectors: 
\begin_inset Formula $g=a\cdot f$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $g(t)=a\cdot f(t)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\Real$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

The upshot of all this is that the set of all real functions is a vector space! 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

But we are really more interested in a subspace of this vector space, i.e., we really want to restrict our attention to the set of all 
\series bold
differentiable
\series default
 functions on some closed interval. 
\end_layout

