 Ordinary Differential Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is linear: 
\begin_inset Formula $D[af+bg]=a[Df] + b[Dg]$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Sometimes we get lax and simply write 
\begin_inset Formula $Df(t)$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $[Df](t)$
\end_inset

. However, keep in mind that 
\begin_inset Formula $Df(t)$
\end_inset

 says "Differentiate 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and then evaluate the resulting function at the point 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

." 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 obeys all the usual rules of differential calculus such as the product rule and the chain rule: 
\begin_inset Formula \[D(fg)=[Df]g+f[Dg]\]
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula \[D(f\circ g) = [Df]\circ g \cdot Dg\]
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

