 Ordinary Differential Operators: Examples 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\id$
\end_inset

 is the identity function, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\id(t)=t$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $D\id=1$
\end_inset

. (You probably know this as 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dt}{dt}=1$
\end_inset

; Note that 1 is the function whose value at every 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 1.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Using the product rule, we have, for example, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align*}
    D\id^2 &= D[\id \cdot \id]\\
           &= D\id \cdot \id + \id \cdot D\id\\
           &= 1 \cdot \id + \id \cdot 1\\
           &= \id + \id\\
           &= 2\id
  \end{align*}
\end_inset

 (In more familiar notation this is just 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dt^2}{dt}=2t$
\end_inset

.) 
\end_layout

