 Ordinary Differential Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Since there is really only one generating operator 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 that we are interested in here, we are less interested in multiplication than we are in function composition or iteration of this operator. For instance, 
\begin_inset Formula $D^2f=[D\circ D](f) = D(Df)$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

By induction we have 
\begin_inset Formula $D^n=\underbrace{D\circ\cdots \circ D}_n$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Just as real-valued functions inherit algebraic operations from those of their range spaces, so too do differential operators: 
\begin_inset Formula \[\left[aD^m+bD^n\right](f)=a(D^mf)+b(D^nf)\]
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

