 Ordinary Differential Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

We can therefore generate polynomials in 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \[p(D)=a_nD^n+\cdots +a_1D+a_oI,\]
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the identity operator on 
\begin_inset Formula $C^\infty[a,b]$
\end_inset

. We usually just drop the identity operator, since it amounts to multiplying by 1. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Such polynomials in 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 are still linear operators. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

Thus, we have rediscovered the group ring 
\begin_inset Formula $\Real(\Diff)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Diff$
\end_inset

 in this case is the infinite cyclic group generated by the differential operator 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

. We'll call this group ring the 
\shape italic

\series bold
polynomial ring of differential operators
\series default

\shape default
. 
\end_layout

